WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.760
So imagine you're sitting in your high school algebra class.

00:03.840 --> 00:05.620
Oh, bringing back the trauma right away, huh?

00:05.780 --> 00:06.440
Right, exactly.

00:06.560 --> 00:07.860
But just, you know, bear with me.

00:08.640 --> 00:14.500
You're staring at this piece of paper, and on it is this incredibly messy, complicated equation.

00:14.820 --> 00:15.060
Right.

00:15.060 --> 00:28.200
And your teacher is standing up in the front, and basically the implicit promise of the whole educational system in that moment is this mathematics is just a machine for calculating the correct answer.

00:28.440 --> 00:28.860
A calculator.

00:28.860 --> 00:30.300
Yeah, a glorified calculator.

00:30.640 --> 00:38.620
You plug a number into the equation, you do a series of mechanical steps, turn the crank, and out pops like a single undeniable truth.

00:38.740 --> 00:38.920
Right.

00:39.180 --> 00:40.700
And if you get the right number, you win.

00:40.960 --> 00:41.320
Exactly.

00:41.480 --> 00:43.300
And if you get the wrong number, you fail.

00:43.680 --> 00:47.500
I mean, that is how almost all of us were taught to think about math, right?

00:48.000 --> 00:50.380
That it's just this exalted form of accounting.

00:50.900 --> 00:52.480
Yeah, the mechanical view of mathematics.

00:52.780 --> 00:57.520
It's incredibly common, and honestly, I think it's comforting for a lot of people.

00:57.600 --> 00:57.900
Comforting.

00:57.900 --> 00:58.420
How so?

00:58.420 --> 01:04.140
Well, when you treat math as just a glorified calculator, you get to live in a very binary world.

01:04.300 --> 01:08.580
There's a definitive input, a definitive output, and a very clear path between them.

01:08.660 --> 01:08.740
Right.

01:08.800 --> 01:09.600
There's no ambiguity.

01:09.600 --> 01:10.840
Exactly.

01:10.840 --> 01:14.880
But today we're going to completely shatter that illusion.

01:14.880 --> 01:25.740
We really are, because when you step away from that high school chalkboard and look at the absolute bleeding edge of modern abstract mathematics, that calculator isn't just broken.

01:25.740 --> 01:26.740
It's entirely irrelevant.

01:26.740 --> 01:28.740
It's entirely irrelevant.

01:28.740 --> 01:29.740
Completely irrelevant.

01:29.740 --> 01:30.180
Yeah.

01:30.180 --> 01:35.500
So what if I told you that mathematics isn't actually about calculating the right answer at all?

01:35.500 --> 01:42.020
What if, at its very core, math is actually the rigorous study of what we can tell apart?

01:42.020 --> 01:47.840
Moving the focus from computation to distinguishability, I mean, that is a massive paradigm shift right there.

01:47.840 --> 01:48.480
It really is.

01:48.480 --> 01:51.640
It completely changes the definition of what a mathematician is actually doing.

01:51.640 --> 01:53.800
And we are going to explore exactly what that means.

01:54.260 --> 01:57.420
So today's Deep Diet is centered on this truly mind-bending manuscript.

01:57.840 --> 02:01.480
It was published in July 2026 by an author named Flyxion.

02:01.760 --> 02:02.000
Right.

02:02.240 --> 02:03.660
The Geometry of Distinction.

02:04.640 --> 02:08.760
Information, representation, and structure across mathematics.

02:08.980 --> 02:09.440
That's the one.

02:09.440 --> 02:25.880
And our mission today is to take the dense, profound thesis of this manuscript and trace a single unbroken thread from that basic algebra you remember all the way to, like, chaos theory, category theory, and information theory.

02:26.020 --> 02:26.680
It's quite a journey.

02:26.860 --> 02:27.200
It is.

02:27.340 --> 02:33.200
We're going to prove to you that almost all of modern mathematics boils down to just three fundamental actions.

02:33.200 --> 02:40.680
Just three actions that govern, you know, everything from how your computer compresses a file to how the universe organizes itself.

02:40.800 --> 02:40.920
Right.

02:41.260 --> 02:44.940
Because according to the text, whenever you are doing math, you're doing one of three things.

02:45.040 --> 02:51.800
You're either preserving distinctions, you're deliberately collapsing distinctions, or you are simply changing how those distinctions are represented.

02:51.920 --> 02:52.140
Okay.

02:52.260 --> 02:57.900
So I was trying to wrap my head around this three-action framework earlier, and I came up with an analogy to sort of ground us.

02:57.920 --> 02:58.440
Let's hear it.

02:58.440 --> 03:06.560
Okay, if you're listening to this, I want you to think of math not as a calculator, but as a massive filing system for reality.

03:06.940 --> 03:07.820
A filing system, okay.

03:08.040 --> 03:08.260
Yeah.

03:08.500 --> 03:11.600
Let's say you have a giant stack of physical documents.

03:12.100 --> 03:18.460
Sometimes you need to keep every single document in its own clearly labeled separate folder.

03:18.640 --> 03:22.340
Because you need to know that document A is not document B.

03:22.460 --> 03:22.980
Exactly.

03:23.460 --> 03:24.880
You preserve all the distinctions.

03:25.280 --> 03:26.280
Nothing gets mixed up.

03:26.360 --> 03:27.260
That is action one.

03:27.260 --> 03:28.960
A perfectly organized archive.

03:29.100 --> 03:29.320
Right.

03:29.580 --> 03:35.700
But then there are times when, say, you're doing your taxes, and you have this massive pile of grocery receipts.

03:35.840 --> 03:36.760
Oh, the worst.

03:36.900 --> 03:38.140
Yeah, the absolute worst.

03:38.400 --> 03:45.120
But for your specific tax purposes, you just do not care about the difference between a receipt for apples and a receipt for bread.

03:45.240 --> 03:46.480
They're all just food expenses.

03:46.700 --> 03:47.140
Exactly.

03:47.140 --> 03:51.240
So you intentionally dump them all into one giant shoebox.

03:51.660 --> 03:54.440
You deliberately destroy the differences between them.

03:54.780 --> 03:56.240
You collapse the distinctions.

03:56.780 --> 03:57.860
That is action two.

03:58.060 --> 04:00.400
The intentional forgetting of your relevant details.

04:00.520 --> 04:00.780
Yes.

04:00.960 --> 04:01.180
Okay.

04:01.280 --> 04:02.220
So what's the third action?

04:02.340 --> 04:07.780
Action three is when you take a critical document and you translate it from English to Spanish.

04:08.040 --> 04:12.500
You haven't merged it with another document, and you haven't lost any of its original meaning.

04:12.500 --> 04:14.340
You just change the representation of it.

04:14.560 --> 04:14.720
Right.

04:14.860 --> 04:16.960
So that completely different person can read it.

04:17.340 --> 04:20.380
The structure is identical, but the language has shifted.

04:20.580 --> 04:25.340
That filing system analogy maps perfectly onto the architecture we're about to explore.

04:25.340 --> 04:38.600
Because, you know, whether you're navigating a geometric space or analyzing a probability distribution or training a complex machine learning algorithm, you're constantly asking the same fundamental questions.

04:39.300 --> 04:39.560
Which are?

04:39.600 --> 04:41.920
Which distinctions survive this process?

04:42.400 --> 04:43.260
Which ones are forgotten?

04:44.000 --> 04:46.740
And which ones are merely expressed in a new language?

04:46.920 --> 04:51.500
So let's start with the first action and look at the actual engines that drive these changes.

04:51.660 --> 04:53.140
I want to talk about functions.

04:53.140 --> 04:54.700
Ah, functions.

04:55.340 --> 04:56.020
The classic.

04:56.200 --> 04:56.400
Right.

04:56.480 --> 05:00.460
Because, again, I was taught that a function is just a rule, like a factory machine.

05:00.640 --> 05:04.760
You put an X on the conveyor belt, the machine hammers on it, and a Y comes out the other side.

05:04.820 --> 05:04.980
Right.

05:05.060 --> 05:06.580
The classical input-output mapping.

05:07.120 --> 05:10.700
And it's a perfectly functional way to teach basic algebra.

05:11.100 --> 05:11.440
But?

05:11.740 --> 05:17.020
But the text argues that it's intentionally economical to the point of being deceptive.

05:17.280 --> 05:17.720
Deceptive.

05:17.880 --> 05:18.120
Wow.

05:18.120 --> 05:21.020
Yeah, because it tells you the mechanics of the evaluation, sure.

05:21.740 --> 05:26.480
But it completely conceals what the function is fundamentally accomplishing on a structural level.

05:26.700 --> 05:28.040
Okay, so how should we look at it?

05:28.580 --> 05:32.640
To really grasp modern math, you have to pivot to a much richer view.

05:33.580 --> 05:40.920
Imagine a function not as a machine, but as a geological force transforming an entire landscape of distinct states.

05:40.920 --> 05:42.420
A landscape of states.

05:42.600 --> 05:53.740
Okay, so instead of one X on a conveyor belt, I need to visualize a massive field where every single point on the ground is a completely distinct input.

05:53.740 --> 05:54.940
Yes, exactly.

05:55.620 --> 06:02.780
Before you apply any mathematical function, every element in your starting space, what we call the domain, is completely distinct from every other element.

06:02.900 --> 06:03.980
They all have their own identity.

06:04.260 --> 06:05.860
Right, they possess their own sovereign identity.

06:06.260 --> 06:12.000
But when the function acts on that landscape, it forces those points to move to a new landscape, the target space.

06:12.080 --> 06:12.860
Okay, I'm with you.

06:13.040 --> 06:18.960
And the first major possibility is that the function preserves every single distinction from the original landscape.

06:18.960 --> 06:25.760
So if no two inputs ever land on the exact same output, the text refers to this as an injective function.

06:26.040 --> 06:26.540
Injective.

06:26.640 --> 06:28.180
Okay, so that lines up with action one.

06:28.940 --> 06:31.200
My documents all stay in separate folders.

06:31.820 --> 06:32.960
Nothing gets mixed up.

06:33.240 --> 06:33.720
Exactly.

06:34.000 --> 06:39.180
The critical feature of an injective function is that no information is ever destroyed.

06:39.940 --> 06:47.480
Because every single output traces back to exactly one unique input, you can always reverse engineer the process.

06:47.480 --> 06:49.600
Perfect reconstruction is possible.

06:49.800 --> 06:50.000
Right.

06:50.180 --> 06:53.460
You haven't lost the ability to distinguish between your starting points.

06:53.620 --> 06:59.280
But the function might not actually reach all the possible points in the new landscape, right?

06:59.400 --> 07:03.160
Like, it might preserve all my documents, but only fill up half the filing cabinet.

07:03.320 --> 07:04.220
That is a great point.

07:04.480 --> 07:08.000
And it introduces the second major concept, surjective function.

07:08.120 --> 07:08.640
Surjective.

07:08.740 --> 07:08.880
Right.

07:08.980 --> 07:11.920
Surjectivity does not care about preserving distinctions.

07:12.440 --> 07:13.580
It only cares about coverage.

07:13.580 --> 07:18.540
A surjective function exhausts the absolute potential of the target space.

07:18.800 --> 07:19.720
So it hits everything.

07:20.000 --> 07:24.300
It guarantees that every single possible output is hit by at least one input.

07:24.560 --> 07:30.340
Okay, so if a function manages to do both, if it perfectly preserves every distinction so nothing is mixed up,

07:30.580 --> 07:34.040
and it hits every single possible output in the new space.

07:34.400 --> 07:36.760
Then you are looking at a bijective function.

07:36.760 --> 07:41.920
It establishes a flawless one-to-one correspondence between the two landscapes.

07:42.040 --> 07:43.760
Nothing is identified with anything else.

07:43.800 --> 07:45.080
And nothing is left unused.

07:45.260 --> 07:45.460
Right.

07:45.600 --> 07:48.080
It is the gold standard of perfect reversibility.

07:48.220 --> 07:50.040
See, this is where my intuition hits a wall.

07:50.420 --> 07:50.960
How so?

07:51.180 --> 07:56.080
Well, if projective functions are this perfect reversible ideal where no data is lost,

07:56.660 --> 07:59.660
aren't non-injective functions just fundamentally broken?

07:59.900 --> 08:00.240
Broken.

08:00.240 --> 08:07.720
If a function forces two distinct inputs to crash into the exact same output, it destroys information.

08:08.320 --> 08:13.120
Why would mathematicians spend so much time studying functions that actively destroy information?

08:13.600 --> 08:18.880
That instinct right there, that information loss, is a deficiency or a flaw that's incredibly common.

08:19.280 --> 08:22.700
But the text flips that assumption completely upside down.

08:22.840 --> 08:23.160
Really?

08:23.480 --> 08:23.760
Yes.

08:24.060 --> 08:26.580
In mathematics, information loss is not a mistake.

08:27.080 --> 08:29.320
It is a highly structured, intentional tool.

08:29.320 --> 08:34.640
When a function collapses distinctions, it doesn't just randomly mash things together.

08:34.960 --> 08:36.040
It does so systematically.

08:36.320 --> 08:37.920
Systematically collapsing things.

08:38.120 --> 08:38.360
Okay.

08:38.560 --> 08:41.300
To explain this, the author introduces the concept of fibers.

08:41.780 --> 08:42.420
Fibers.

08:43.000 --> 08:45.040
Like threads connecting the two landscapes.

08:45.360 --> 08:47.060
That's actually a good way to visualize it.

08:48.140 --> 08:55.860
Think of a fiber as a collection of inputs from your starting landscape that all map to the exact same output.

08:55.860 --> 09:00.280
So if my function isn't injective, multiple inputs are colliding.

09:00.580 --> 09:00.880
Right.

09:01.280 --> 09:04.100
The fiber is the family of all those colliding points.

09:04.540 --> 09:13.160
The function has made a deliberate mathematical choice that, for its specific purposes, those points are completely indistinguishable.

09:13.160 --> 09:17.020
Let me test this with an example from the source material, just to make sure I'm getting it.

09:17.080 --> 09:18.680
The absolute value function.

09:18.940 --> 09:20.100
Ah, perfect example.

09:20.280 --> 09:22.920
If I take the absolute value of 5, the answer is 5.

09:23.020 --> 09:26.420
If I take the absolute value of negative 5, the answer is also 5.

09:26.600 --> 09:28.760
A beautifully simple illustration of a fiber.

09:28.760 --> 09:32.000
So the absolute value function creates a collision.

09:32.480 --> 09:35.740
It takes 5 and negative 5 and collapses them into the exact same output.

09:35.840 --> 09:36.660
It has a blind spot.

09:36.940 --> 09:37.220
Exactly.

09:37.340 --> 09:38.360
A mathematical blind spot.

09:38.560 --> 09:43.680
It completely ignores the sign of the number, whether it's on the positive side of the number line or the negative side.

09:43.940 --> 09:45.740
But it doesn't forget the magnitude.

09:46.340 --> 09:49.020
The distance from zero is preserved perfectly.

09:49.480 --> 09:54.960
And that structured forgetting is precisely what makes the absolute value function useful.

09:55.260 --> 09:56.360
Because it's intentional.

09:56.360 --> 10:03.600
Right. It intentionally declares that the distinction between positive and negative is irrelevant for whatever distance you were trying to calculate.

10:04.180 --> 10:08.300
You're intentionally throwing away the sign to focus purely on the magnitude.

10:08.600 --> 10:09.740
Okay. That makes sense.

10:09.860 --> 10:15.600
In linear algebra, this concept of the mathematical blind spot is formalized into something called the kernel.

10:16.000 --> 10:19.180
The kernel. That sounds intimidating. How does that work in geometry?

10:19.520 --> 10:20.700
It's not as scary as it sounds.

10:20.700 --> 10:27.100
Imagine you have a three-dimensional object, like a cube, and you shine a light on it to create a two-dimensional shadow on the wall.

10:27.240 --> 10:29.200
Okay. 3D cube, 2D shadow.

10:29.520 --> 10:33.900
The transformation from a 3D object to a 2D shadow loses information.

10:34.360 --> 10:35.340
You lose the depth.

10:35.620 --> 10:35.980
Right.

10:36.340 --> 10:40.100
The kernel is the formal mathematical description of that lost depth.

10:40.840 --> 10:44.200
It is the set of all directions that the transformation simply cannot see.

10:44.340 --> 10:45.600
Because it's blind to them.

10:45.600 --> 10:53.260
Yes. Any movement strictly along the direction of the kernel produces absolutely zero observable change in the output shadow.

10:53.680 --> 10:58.400
It is a precise geometric measurement of the transformation's blindness.

10:59.020 --> 11:07.260
Okay. So if every function has a specific blind spot and a specific way it collapses information, how does that help us understand math as a whole?

11:07.260 --> 11:12.600
Oh, it leads to one of the most profound structural realizations in all of algebra.

11:13.100 --> 11:16.640
The text leans heavily on this. It's called the first isomorphism theorem.

11:17.060 --> 11:22.620
The first isomorphism theorem. The text treats this almost like a holy grail. Let's unpack it. What does it actually do?

11:22.860 --> 11:27.680
It reveals a universal architecture hiding inside every single mathematical transformation.

11:28.340 --> 11:36.520
The theorem proves that every transformation you can possibly imagine is not a single action, but secretly a two-step process.

11:36.520 --> 11:38.540
Two steps. Okay. Walk me through the mechanics of that.

11:38.700 --> 11:40.740
Step one is the irreversible collapse.

11:40.900 --> 11:41.420
The collapse.

11:41.600 --> 11:48.580
The function surveys its domain, identifies every single distinction that lives inside its blind spot as kernel, and deliberately crushes them.

11:48.720 --> 11:50.120
So it forces them together.

11:50.580 --> 11:58.920
Yes. It groups all the indistinguishable elements into clusters, forming a new compressed space called a quotient.

11:59.040 --> 12:01.900
So the quotient is the shoebox for my receipts.

12:02.020 --> 12:02.480
Exactly.

12:02.480 --> 12:11.060
The function says, I am blind to the difference between an apple receipt and a bread receipt, so I am going to compress them both into a single object called food expense.

12:11.400 --> 12:18.820
The math actually supports your intuition perfectly there. That is the collapse. You are quotienting out the irrelevant information.

12:18.960 --> 12:19.860
Okay. And step two.

12:19.860 --> 12:29.300
Step two. Once you have collapsed, the invisible directions, once you've formed your shoeboxes, what you are left with is a highly compressed structure.

12:29.720 --> 12:38.020
And the theorem guarantees that this new compressed structure can be embedded perfectly with absolute faithful preservation into the target space.

12:38.140 --> 12:40.200
Oh, wow. So it's a massive bait and switch.

12:40.460 --> 12:40.820
How do you mean?

12:40.860 --> 12:44.100
Step one is aggressively destroy all the information you can't see.

12:44.240 --> 12:48.020
Step two is take whatever survives that purge and preserve it flawlessly.

12:48.020 --> 12:56.020
That is exactly it. Every homomorphism in algebra, every linear transformation in geometry follows this exact blueprint.

12:56.720 --> 12:59.960
Collapse the invisible, then faithfully represent the remainder.

13:00.420 --> 13:01.840
That is fascinating.

13:02.080 --> 13:08.300
And once you understand this, it naturally generates a cosmic accounting system for information,

13:08.740 --> 13:12.800
which in linear algebra is formalized as the Rankin-Nullity theorem.

13:12.800 --> 13:18.800
The conservation of degrees of freedom. I read this section three times because it felt so, I don't know, so satisfying.

13:19.040 --> 13:24.800
It is deeply satisfying because it acts as a fundamental law of conservation for mathematical structure.

13:25.200 --> 13:32.160
It dictates that the total number of dimensions you start with must equal the dimensions of what survives, which we call the Rank,

13:32.520 --> 13:36.100
plus the dimensions of what disappears into the kernel, which we call the Nullity.

13:36.320 --> 13:39.540
Rank plus Nullity. So nothing is magically lost to the void.

13:39.540 --> 13:44.520
And nothing is magically created out of thin air. If you start with a five-dimensional space,

13:44.740 --> 13:48.720
every single one of those independent directions experiences exactly one of two fates.

13:48.920 --> 13:49.040
Right.

13:49.120 --> 13:56.780
It either survives the process as an observable degree of freedom, or it falls into the blind spot and vanishes into the kernel.

13:57.140 --> 13:59.540
You can account for every single drop of information.

14:00.220 --> 14:02.540
Which naturally pushes us toward a much bigger question.

14:02.880 --> 14:04.480
I feel like there's always a bigger question.

14:04.480 --> 14:15.560
There is. If this two-step architecture, this rhythm of collapse, followed by faithful embedding, applies to numbers, and to geometric vectors, and to symmetries.

14:15.720 --> 14:19.200
Is there a master language that governs all of them?

14:19.220 --> 14:25.900
Exactly. Is there a way to study the architecture itself without getting bogged down in whether we're talking about shapes or numbers?

14:25.900 --> 14:31.800
And that question pulls us out of the realm of classical algebra and drops us straight into category theory.

14:31.920 --> 14:32.600
Category theory.

14:32.760 --> 14:36.980
Which, from what I gather, requires us to completely unlearn how we look at objects.

14:37.180 --> 14:37.520
It does.

14:37.900 --> 14:41.240
Category theory represents a radical philosophical pivot.

14:42.220 --> 14:45.700
Traditionally, if you want to define a mathematical object, you dissect it.

14:45.920 --> 14:47.240
You look at its internal parts.

14:47.320 --> 14:48.220
Like what's inside it.

14:48.620 --> 14:48.900
Right.

14:48.900 --> 14:52.740
What are the specific elements inside this set?

14:53.160 --> 14:55.280
What do the vectors in this space look like?

14:55.720 --> 15:00.200
But category theory argues that looking inside the object is a trap.

15:00.520 --> 15:00.880
A trap.

15:01.120 --> 15:01.380
Yes.

15:01.900 --> 15:07.080
You cannot truly understand an object by dissecting its internal organs.

15:07.600 --> 15:09.060
Let me try out an analogy here.

15:09.140 --> 15:10.400
Tell me if I'm on track.

15:10.600 --> 15:11.020
Go for it.

15:11.180 --> 15:13.940
Imagine you're trying to define who I am as a human being.

15:14.560 --> 15:18.680
The traditional classical mathematical approach would be to put me under a microscope.

15:19.020 --> 15:19.660
Dissect you.

15:19.940 --> 15:20.160
Right.

15:20.340 --> 15:21.720
You'd look at my cellular biology.

15:21.900 --> 15:28.160
You'd say, this host is composed of this specific sequence of DNA, these carbon atoms, this organ structure.

15:28.280 --> 15:28.780
They're analytical.

15:29.080 --> 15:33.220
But category theory says, no, that doesn't tell us anything useful about who you are in the world.

15:33.620 --> 15:36.800
To define you, we need to look at your network of relationships.

15:37.220 --> 15:38.340
Who do you love?

15:38.460 --> 15:39.340
Who do you work with?

15:39.340 --> 15:40.440
Who do you owe money to?

15:40.860 --> 15:42.980
Your identity isn't what you're made of.

15:42.980 --> 15:47.220
Your identity is entirely determined by your position in the broader network.

15:47.600 --> 15:48.480
Look at it from this angle.

15:48.860 --> 15:52.740
That analogy captures the exact mathematical mechanics of a category.

15:53.080 --> 15:53.340
Yes.

15:53.840 --> 15:57.600
In this framework, those relationships you just described are called morphisms.

15:58.060 --> 16:03.640
The mathematical objects themselves, the sets, the spaces, become almost passive.

16:03.740 --> 16:04.300
Passive.

16:04.360 --> 16:05.380
Like they don't do anything.

16:05.620 --> 16:08.340
They're simply the nodes where morphisms intersect.

16:08.540 --> 16:08.800
Yeah.

16:08.800 --> 16:16.620
The morphisms, the transformations, the arrows pointing between the objects are elevated to become the true fundamental actors of mathematics.

16:16.860 --> 16:19.520
But wait, I have a major logistical problem with this.

16:19.600 --> 16:19.800
Okay.

16:19.900 --> 16:20.340
What is it?

16:20.340 --> 16:23.740
If we throw away the internal elements, how do we actually do the math?

16:24.080 --> 16:31.560
Earlier, we defined an injective function by saying, if I take two distinct inputs, they don't map to the same output.

16:31.920 --> 16:33.020
I had to look at the inputs.

16:33.180 --> 16:33.300
Right.

16:33.300 --> 16:40.560
If category theory makes it illegal to look at the individual elements, how can I possibly prove that a function preserves information?

16:41.100 --> 16:45.580
This is exactly where category theory flexes its muscles and shows its brilliance.

16:45.820 --> 16:51.900
It rebuilds the entire concept of injectivity from scratch using nothing but the network of relationships.

16:52.100 --> 16:52.640
Just the arrows.

16:52.720 --> 16:53.300
Just the arrows.

16:53.440 --> 16:53.600
Yeah.

16:53.600 --> 16:55.920
It introduces a concept called a monomorphism.

16:56.760 --> 16:57.320
Monomorphism.

16:57.500 --> 16:59.380
Break down the mechanics of that for me.

16:59.640 --> 17:03.140
How do you prove distinguishability without looking inside?

17:03.520 --> 17:10.760
Instead of asking if a function, let's call it f, can distinguish between two individual elements inside a set,

17:11.180 --> 17:15.920
we ask if it can distinguish between two entirely different processes that enter the set.

17:16.100 --> 17:16.360
Okay.

17:16.500 --> 17:17.560
I need to visualize this.

17:17.620 --> 17:22.020
So instead of elements sitting inside a circle, I'm looking at arrows pointing into the circle.

17:22.180 --> 17:22.480
Yes.

17:22.480 --> 17:27.320
Suppose you have two completely different mathematical transformations, let's call them g and h,

17:27.540 --> 17:29.440
and they're both pointing into your object.

17:29.520 --> 17:29.860
Got it.

17:30.020 --> 17:31.540
G and h are pointing in.

17:31.620 --> 17:34.880
Now, you apply your function f to the results of both.

17:35.560 --> 17:43.460
If applying f afterward makes the final results identical, meaning f composed with g looks exactly the same as f composed with h,

17:43.940 --> 17:48.240
does that force us to conclude that g and h were actually the same process all along?

17:48.320 --> 17:49.480
Let me think about that.

17:49.480 --> 17:52.540
If the answer is yes, then f is a monomorphism.

17:52.720 --> 17:53.720
I think I see it.

17:53.720 --> 18:01.040
It means f is so perfectly transparent that it never, ever destroys the distinctions that g and h created before it.

18:01.040 --> 18:03.780
If g and h were different, f keeps them different.

18:03.780 --> 18:06.880
It preserves the information of the entire network.

18:07.280 --> 18:09.640
In formal terms, we say the function is left cancelable.

18:10.100 --> 18:15.620
And if you apply the exact same logic in reverse, you get the categorical version of surge activity.

18:16.040 --> 18:16.820
Let me guess.

18:17.320 --> 18:20.160
We look at arrows pointing out of the object instead of in.

18:20.160 --> 18:22.020
You are tracking the logic perfectly.

18:22.740 --> 18:24.380
It is called an epimorphism.

18:24.980 --> 18:25.540
Epimorphism.

18:25.740 --> 18:34.500
If a function f provides such complete coverage of its target space that it can uniquely determine the behavior of any future transformation that follows it,

18:34.900 --> 18:36.020
it's an epimorphism.

18:36.220 --> 18:37.260
It is right cancelable.

18:37.460 --> 18:43.300
So we have successfully replaced the microscopic study of elements and points with the macroscopic geometry of arrows.

18:43.500 --> 18:43.940
We have.

18:43.940 --> 18:49.340
And speaking of the geometry of arrows, the source material spends pages dissecting commutative diagrams,

18:49.840 --> 18:56.520
which, to the untrained eye, look like someone drew a complex corporate flowchart and tried to pass it off as math.

18:56.680 --> 18:58.040
They do look like flowcharts.

18:58.560 --> 19:00.660
But they are incredibly rigorous.

19:01.340 --> 19:06.720
In advanced mathematics, you eventually stop writing out long, horizontal strings of algebraic equations.

19:06.780 --> 19:07.660
You just draw pictures.

19:08.200 --> 19:08.600
Basically.

19:08.860 --> 19:09.780
You draw a map.

19:09.960 --> 19:12.860
You draw a diagram of objects connected by arrows.

19:12.860 --> 19:19.100
If the mathematician declares that the diagram commutes, it is a powerful statement.

19:19.240 --> 19:20.960
What does it mean for a diagram to commute?

19:21.280 --> 19:29.680
It means that no matter which physical path of arrows you follow, from object A to object B, the final resulting transformation is identical.

19:29.880 --> 19:31.220
So it's like navigating a city.

19:31.480 --> 19:31.880
Yes.

19:32.180 --> 19:38.000
If I start at my apartment and I want to get to a specific coffee shop, I can walk three blocks north and two blocks east.

19:38.380 --> 19:41.500
Or I can walk two blocks east and three blocks north.

19:41.500 --> 19:45.800
It doesn't matter which path of arrows I take, I arrive at the exact same coffee shop.

19:46.000 --> 19:47.120
The diagram commutes.

19:47.320 --> 19:53.100
And the geometry of that diagram completely replaces the need for the algebraic equation.

19:53.380 --> 19:53.580
Yeah.

19:53.940 --> 19:57.520
But I sense you have a reservation about this level of abstraction.

19:57.660 --> 19:58.060
I do.

19:58.180 --> 19:59.420
I have a massive reservation.

19:59.740 --> 20:00.500
Lay it on me.

20:00.840 --> 20:03.800
Doesn't this just make math needlessly impossibly complicated?

20:03.800 --> 20:05.680
I mean, we started with numbers, which makes sense.

20:05.780 --> 20:07.940
Then we abstracted to variables, then to functions.

20:08.460 --> 20:14.120
And now we are dealing with abstract networks of arrows where we don't even know what the objects are made of.

20:14.240 --> 20:16.520
And we're just tracing paths on a flow chart.

20:17.300 --> 20:18.840
Why would anyone do this to themselves?

20:18.980 --> 20:20.740
Aren't we just hiding the actual math?

20:20.740 --> 20:24.460
It absolutely feels more complicated when you first encounter it.

20:24.620 --> 20:24.700
Yeah.

20:24.800 --> 20:27.000
It feels like you were losing your grip on reality.

20:27.300 --> 20:27.500
Totally.

20:27.860 --> 20:34.140
But the author addresses this exact friction with a quote that is arguably the thesis of the entire manuscript.

20:35.080 --> 20:35.900
The text states,

20:36.360 --> 20:38.260
Abstraction is not the removal of meaning.

20:38.800 --> 20:41.320
It is the removal of unnecessary detail.

20:41.520 --> 20:43.320
The removal of unnecessary detail.

20:43.480 --> 20:44.280
That sounds nice.

20:44.320 --> 20:46.780
But how does that actually help solve a problem?

20:46.780 --> 20:50.260
Think of abstraction purely as data compression for the human mind.

20:51.040 --> 20:52.640
Let's say you are a classical mathematician.

20:53.560 --> 20:54.800
You study the theory of sets.

20:55.540 --> 20:57.060
Then you study vector spaces.

20:57.500 --> 20:58.340
Then groups.

20:58.900 --> 21:00.140
Then topological spaces.

21:00.260 --> 21:00.360
Okay.

21:00.480 --> 21:01.360
Four different fields.

21:01.560 --> 21:02.560
In each of those fields,

21:02.820 --> 21:06.640
you are going to discover a version of the first isomorphism theorem.

21:06.820 --> 21:07.040
Right.

21:07.120 --> 21:08.880
The collapse and embed thing we talked about.

21:09.320 --> 21:09.840
Exactly.

21:10.080 --> 21:16.320
So you have to rigorously prove and memorize that same foundational theorem 16 different times

21:16.320 --> 21:19.300
using 16 completely different sets of vocabulary.

21:19.540 --> 21:20.540
That sounds miserable.

21:20.860 --> 21:22.440
It is incredibly inefficient.

21:22.820 --> 21:27.200
It's like trying to store a massive computer file that just says the letter A a million times.

21:27.320 --> 21:28.540
Oh, I see where you're going with this.

21:29.200 --> 21:31.360
Category theory is the compression algorithm.

21:32.080 --> 21:36.760
It strips away the specific vocabulary of geometry or algebra or topology.

21:36.760 --> 21:39.940
It looks at the underlying network of arrows and says,

21:40.460 --> 21:42.060
Just store the rule 16A.

21:42.700 --> 21:43.180
Wow.

21:43.180 --> 21:46.840
You prove one universal categorical theorem about how arrows behave,

21:47.080 --> 21:52.740
and that single proof automatically applies to all 16 distinct mathematical disciplines.

21:53.380 --> 21:55.200
It isn't making the math harder.

21:55.820 --> 22:00.240
It's revealing that there are actually an incredibly small number of structural themes

22:00.240 --> 22:01.880
running the entire universe.

22:02.320 --> 22:03.880
That is a brilliant way to frame it.

22:04.040 --> 22:08.660
Category theory strips away the specific language to expose the raw universal structure.

22:08.660 --> 22:13.320
But what if the structure is fine, and it's the language itself that's the problem?

22:13.380 --> 22:13.720
What do you mean?

22:13.880 --> 22:18.180
Like, what if I'm stuck trying to solve a problem in mathematical English,

22:18.580 --> 22:22.580
but the problem desperately wants to be solved in mathematical Spanish?

22:23.200 --> 22:23.520
Oh.

22:23.760 --> 22:27.300
That brings us to the third action of math translation without loss.

22:27.560 --> 22:30.720
This is where we leave the folders and the shoeboxes behind,

22:30.720 --> 22:34.620
and we look at how we represent the distinctions we've decided to keep.

22:34.620 --> 22:40.020
The text begins this exploration by examining something I thought was completely basic coordinates.

22:40.300 --> 22:42.520
I always assumed that coordinates were the geometry.

22:42.900 --> 22:43.560
A lot of people do.

22:43.940 --> 22:46.920
Like, if I put a point on a graph at 3,4,

22:47.060 --> 22:49.900
those numbers are the fundamental truth of where that point is.

22:50.260 --> 22:53.560
But the text argues that coordinates are just an arbitrary dictionary.

22:53.900 --> 22:55.500
It's a crucial conceptual leap.

22:56.060 --> 22:58.660
A geometric point exists in physical space,

22:58.780 --> 23:02.260
regardless of how you, the observer, choose to describe it.

23:02.380 --> 23:02.660
Right.

23:02.660 --> 23:06.280
You can describe its location using standard Cartesian coordinates

23:06.280 --> 23:09.980
moving along a rigid x and y axis to get to 3,4.

23:10.640 --> 23:13.980
Or you could describe that exact same point using polar coordinates,

23:14.240 --> 23:17.360
where you define an angle from the center and a distance outward.

23:17.600 --> 23:18.800
But the point hasn't moved.

23:19.080 --> 23:21.360
The physical point hasn't moved a single millimeter.

23:22.060 --> 23:25.980
You have simply chosen a different dictionary to communicate its location.

23:26.320 --> 23:28.180
And because the point hasn't moved,

23:28.620 --> 23:31.820
changing the coordinates is a flawless translation.

23:31.820 --> 23:33.620
You haven't collapsed any distinctions.

23:34.040 --> 23:36.600
You haven't lost a single drop of information.

23:37.080 --> 23:37.760
None at all.

23:38.080 --> 23:41.800
And when you scale this concept up in linear algebra,

23:42.180 --> 23:43.800
it becomes the theory of basis.

23:44.600 --> 23:49.260
A basis is simply the foundational language you choose to construct your vector space.

23:49.720 --> 23:49.980
Okay.

23:50.080 --> 23:51.720
So if you change the basis.

23:51.800 --> 23:52.760
If you change the basis,

23:53.280 --> 23:56.340
the geometric vectors themselves do not warp or bend.

23:56.340 --> 24:01.600
But every single numerical coordinate you use to describe them completely changes.

24:01.960 --> 24:04.660
Which fundamentally alters how we have to think about matrices.

24:05.160 --> 24:08.360
Because a matrix is just a grid of numbers representing a transformation.

24:08.900 --> 24:12.080
But those numbers are completely dependent on the language we are using, right?

24:12.180 --> 24:14.920
That is the core insight of representation theory.

24:14.920 --> 24:18.840
It implies that you can have two completely different grids of numbers.

24:19.140 --> 24:25.140
Two matrices that look absolutely nothing alike that are actually representing the exact same physical transformation.

24:25.480 --> 24:26.480
They're just speaking different languages.

24:26.900 --> 24:28.320
They're just speaking in different bases.

24:28.620 --> 24:29.900
We call them similar matrices.

24:30.200 --> 24:30.400
Okay.

24:30.460 --> 24:35.760
But if I can choose literally any language, any basis to describe my transformation,

24:36.140 --> 24:38.180
how do I know which one to pick?

24:38.260 --> 24:41.180
Is there a best language for a given problem?

24:41.180 --> 24:41.700
Yes.

24:42.380 --> 24:48.480
And the mathematical process of finding that perfect optimal language is called diagonalization.

24:49.020 --> 24:51.180
We need to spend some time on diagonalization.

24:51.640 --> 24:55.840
The text describes it as finding the natural language of a linear operator.

24:56.000 --> 24:56.900
The natural language, right.

24:56.920 --> 25:02.640
If I look at a standard matrix, it's usually a messy, dense grid of numbers.

25:02.920 --> 25:07.640
It implies that every single coordinate is tangled up and interacting with every other coordinate.

25:07.640 --> 25:11.220
Diagonalization somehow untangles that knot.

25:11.580 --> 25:12.960
How does it physically do that?

25:13.100 --> 25:18.140
It untangles the system by searching for very specific hidden directions called eigenvectors.

25:18.600 --> 25:19.200
Eigendector.

25:19.340 --> 25:23.780
To understand this, imagine a transformation that stretches and rotates a geometric space.

25:24.640 --> 25:28.180
Most vectors in that space are going to get knocked off their original path.

25:28.720 --> 25:29.700
They will change direction.

25:29.760 --> 25:31.260
Because the whole space is spinning.

25:31.380 --> 25:31.540
Yeah.

25:31.540 --> 25:32.060
Exactly.

25:32.700 --> 25:35.380
But the eigenvectors are immune to the rotation.

25:35.740 --> 25:37.720
They stay on their exact original line.

25:37.860 --> 25:39.560
They only stretch or shrink.

25:40.020 --> 25:42.160
The amount they stretch is called their eigenvalue.

25:42.360 --> 25:47.700
So they are the pure independent modes of the transformation, the parts that don't get mixed up.

25:47.780 --> 25:48.180
Exactly.

25:48.300 --> 25:48.480
Yeah.

25:48.600 --> 25:50.680
If you can change your language, change your basis,

25:50.900 --> 25:54.860
so that your entire coordinate system is built exclusively out of these eigenvectors,

25:55.140 --> 25:57.740
something magical happens to your messy matrix.

25:57.920 --> 25:58.440
What happens?

25:58.760 --> 26:00.320
All the chaotic interactions vanish.

26:00.320 --> 26:05.020
The matrix collapses into a beautifully simple diagonal line of numbers.

26:05.480 --> 26:12.100
The complex, tangled system is revealed to be just a collection of independent, isolated, stretching actions.

26:12.320 --> 26:15.480
I have an analogy for this that might help listeners visualize it.

26:15.560 --> 26:15.740
All right.

26:16.400 --> 26:20.780
Imagine you are at a concert, listening to a massive symphony orchestra.

26:21.000 --> 26:21.260
Okay.

26:21.460 --> 26:25.940
If you were to record that symphony and look at the raw sound wave on a computer, it's a nightmare.

26:25.940 --> 26:34.040
It's a single, tangled, chaotic mess of noise where the violins, the cellos, the brass, and the percussion are all blended into one line.

26:34.560 --> 26:36.840
That raw sound wave is your messy matrix.

26:36.960 --> 26:37.960
The tangled representation.

26:38.500 --> 26:38.820
Right.

26:39.360 --> 26:44.140
Diagonalization is like a mathematical process that suddenly hands you the conductor's sheet music.

26:44.140 --> 26:49.200
It allows you to perfectly isolate the violin frequency from the trumpet frequency.

26:49.340 --> 26:49.840
Oh, that's good.

26:50.100 --> 26:51.780
The physical music hasn't changed.

26:52.080 --> 26:54.040
The symphony is exactly the same.

26:54.380 --> 27:00.420
But your representation of it has shifted, making all the independent fundamental parts crystal clear.

27:00.420 --> 27:04.460
That is a phenomenal analogy for spectral decomposition.

27:04.980 --> 27:10.140
You are isolating the fundamental, uncoupled frequencies of a system.

27:10.260 --> 27:11.940
It just makes it so much easier to understand.

27:12.360 --> 27:17.760
And the beauty of mathematics is that this doesn't just work for finite, discrete grids of numbers.

27:17.960 --> 27:27.460
If we take your orchestra analogy and push it to infinity, if we apply this logic to continuous functions, we arrive at one of the most powerful tools ever invented.

27:27.640 --> 27:28.740
The Fourier transform.

27:29.040 --> 27:29.940
The Fourier transform.

27:29.940 --> 27:31.600
The ultimate act of translation.

27:31.800 --> 27:35.940
When I first encountered this, it felt less like math and more like sorcery.

27:36.060 --> 27:38.340
It is the mathematical engine of the modern world.

27:38.700 --> 27:49.780
The Fourier transform takes a function that is spread out over time, what we call the time domain, where events happen sequentially in a tangled mess, and it translates it completely into the frequency domain.

27:49.900 --> 27:51.520
So it shifts the perspective.

27:51.520 --> 27:59.620
It shifts the question from when do these events happen to what fundamental frequencies make up this event.

27:59.940 --> 28:01.940
So it does exactly what my symphony analogy did.

28:01.940 --> 28:08.940
It takes the raw audio wave over time and outputs the exact sheet music of constituent frequencies.

28:08.940 --> 28:14.520
It does, but the true sorcery of the Fourier transform isn't just that it identifies frequencies.

28:14.520 --> 28:20.300
The real power is what happens to the underlying calculus when you shift into this new language.

28:20.300 --> 28:21.300
Explain the mechanism there.

28:21.300 --> 28:24.300
Why does changing the language change the difficulty of the math?

28:24.300 --> 28:28.300
Think about a differential equation in the time domain.

28:28.300 --> 28:33.300
Calculating how a complex system changes over time requires differentiation, calculus.

28:33.300 --> 28:35.300
Which can be really hard.

28:35.300 --> 28:39.300
And for complicated systems, computing derivatives is notoriously brutal.

28:39.300 --> 28:43.300
But let's look at the basic building block of a frequency, a sine wave.

28:43.300 --> 28:45.300
What is the derivative of a sine wave?

28:45.300 --> 28:48.300
It's a cosine wave, but multiplied by the frequency.

28:48.300 --> 28:51.300
The math actually supports your intuition perfectly.

28:51.300 --> 28:57.300
Taking the derivative of a basic wave essentially just pulls the frequency value out to the front.

28:57.300 --> 29:02.300
So if you translate your entire complicated equation into the frequency domain using Fourier,

29:02.300 --> 29:07.300
taking a derivative doing calculus literally turns into simple multiplication.

29:07.300 --> 29:10.300
You just multiply your data by the frequency.

29:10.300 --> 29:15.300
Are you saying that by translating the problem, we completely bypass calculus?

29:15.300 --> 29:17.300
That is exactly what I'm saying.

29:17.300 --> 29:22.300
Incredibly difficult calculus suddenly becomes elementary school algebra.

29:22.300 --> 29:27.300
You solve the complex differential equation using basic arithmetic in the frequency domain,

29:27.300 --> 29:30.300
and when you have the answer, you just translate it back to the time domain.

29:30.300 --> 29:31.300
That is staggering.

29:31.300 --> 29:33.300
The fundamental problem hasn't changed.

29:33.300 --> 29:37.300
But simply by shifting to the natural language of frequencies,

29:37.300 --> 29:39.300
the math essentially solves itself.

29:39.300 --> 29:43.300
It's the ultimate proof that representation matters just as much as computation.

29:43.300 --> 29:47.300
And this realization leads mathematicians to a critical pursuit.

29:47.300 --> 29:50.300
If we can manipulate representations this drastically,

29:50.300 --> 29:54.300
if we can jump from coordinates to diagonal matrices to frequency domains,

29:54.300 --> 29:56.300
what actually stays the same?

29:56.300 --> 29:57.300
The anchor points.

29:57.300 --> 29:59.300
The text calls them invariants.

29:59.300 --> 30:02.300
Invariants are the holy grail of classification.

30:02.300 --> 30:08.300
As representations shift and warp, certain core properties stubbornly refuse to change.

30:08.300 --> 30:09.300
Like what?

30:09.300 --> 30:11.300
The dimension of a space is invariant.

30:11.300 --> 30:16.300
The sum of the diagonal elements in a matrix, called the trace, is invariant.

30:16.300 --> 30:19.300
The eigenvalues we discussed are invariant.

30:19.300 --> 30:24.300
In the field of topology, the number of physical holes in a shape is an invariant.

30:24.300 --> 30:26.300
So they don't care about the language at all?

30:26.300 --> 30:27.300
Not at all.

30:27.300 --> 30:31.300
These properties belong to the absolute truth of the mathematical object itself,

30:31.300 --> 30:34.300
completely independent of the language we use to describe it.

30:34.300 --> 30:38.300
Finding invariants is how mathematicians figure out what is real.

30:38.300 --> 30:40.300
Okay, let's take a breath and shift gears.

30:40.300 --> 30:45.300
Up until now, everything we've talked about, the functions, the categories, the translations,

30:45.300 --> 30:48.300
it all feels very static, like a single snapshot.

30:48.300 --> 30:49.300
Very frozen in time.

30:49.300 --> 30:53.300
But what happens when we unleash these transformations over time?

30:53.300 --> 30:57.300
What happens when a function doesn't just act once, but acts on its own outputs,

30:57.300 --> 30:59.300
creating a continuous feedback loop?

30:59.300 --> 31:06.300
We are moving from the algebra of static objects to the dynamics of process.

31:06.300 --> 31:11.300
When we take a function and feed its output back into itself as a new input

31:11.300 --> 31:15.300
calculating f of f of f of x over and over, we are iterating.

31:15.300 --> 31:16.300
Right.

31:16.300 --> 31:21.300
We are taking a static rule and turning it into a dynamic system that unfolds through time.

31:21.300 --> 31:22.300
Iteration.

31:22.300 --> 31:25.300
Okay, so I start with a point, I apply the rule, I get a new point,

31:25.300 --> 31:28.300
then I immediately apply the exact same rule to that new point.

31:28.300 --> 31:31.300
And the central question of dynamical systems theory is,

31:31.300 --> 31:36.300
what is the ultimate fate of this process after a massive number of iterations?

31:36.300 --> 31:39.300
Does the point wander aimlessly forever?

31:39.300 --> 31:41.300
Does it explode out to infinity?

31:41.300 --> 31:43.300
Or does it settle down into a stable equilibrium?

31:43.300 --> 31:47.300
And this inquiry leads us directly to the concept of contraction mappings

31:47.300 --> 31:49.300
and the Banach fixed point theorem.

31:49.300 --> 31:50.300
It does indeed.

31:50.300 --> 31:51.300
That Banach fixed point theorem.

31:51.300 --> 31:55.300
I have a deeply counterintuitive visualization for this that I want to run by you.

31:55.300 --> 31:56.300
Please do.

31:56.300 --> 32:01.300
Imagine you take a completely accurate, flat paper map of your city.

32:01.300 --> 32:02.300
Okay, a map.

32:02.300 --> 32:05.300
You crumple that map up into a tight, chaotic little ball,

32:05.300 --> 32:08.300
and you drop it on the sidewalk somewhere inside the city limits.

32:08.300 --> 32:11.300
According to the math of the Banach fixed point theorem,

32:11.300 --> 32:15.300
there is exactly one microscopic poo on that crumpled piece of paper

32:15.300 --> 32:21.300
that is currently hovering perfectly over the exact physical location on the ground that it represents.

32:21.300 --> 32:25.300
That is a classic, beautiful topological illustration.

32:25.300 --> 32:27.300
It's so weird to think about.

32:27.300 --> 32:30.300
And the mechanics of why it works are fascinating.

32:30.300 --> 32:33.300
Because the map is a contracted, smaller version of the city,

32:33.300 --> 32:36.300
and because you dropped it entirely within the city limits,

32:36.300 --> 32:41.300
the continuous transformation from the real city to the map city

32:41.300 --> 32:45.300
mathematically guarantees that one point gets mapped strictly to itself.

32:45.300 --> 32:46.300
It cannot escape.

32:46.300 --> 32:48.300
It bends the mind to try and visualize it.

32:48.300 --> 32:52.300
It does, but it is also the backbone of modern computation.

32:52.300 --> 32:57.300
If a mathematical transformation always brings points even slightly closer together,

32:57.300 --> 32:58.300
if it contracts the space,

32:58.300 --> 33:04.300
then iterating that function infinitely will always lock onto one unique, immovable fixed point.

33:04.300 --> 33:05.300
So it's inevitable.

33:05.300 --> 33:06.300
Yes.

33:06.300 --> 33:11.300
This is how algorithms like Google's page rank or gradiate descent in machine learning actually function.

33:11.300 --> 33:13.300
We don't solve the equation.

33:13.300 --> 33:14.300
We just let the system settle.

33:14.300 --> 33:17.300
We don't even try to guess the exact numerical answer.

33:17.300 --> 33:19.300
We set up a contraction mapping,

33:19.300 --> 33:21.300
we feed the messy data into it,

33:21.300 --> 33:23.300
